(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "2608052200")
  (:adr/title "aiueos reaches the wire: the link layer, with frame admission in Kotoba (gap 1 of ADR-2608051200, partially closed)")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-08-05")
  (:adr/tags (vector "aiueos" "network" "virtio-net" "kotoba" "murakumo" "evidence"))
  (:adr/supersedes "")
  (:adr/superseded-by "")
  (:adr/deciders (vector "Jun Kawasaki"))
  (:adr/authoritative true)
  (:adr/source-format "edn")
  (:adr/context
  "ADR-2608051200 shipped a USB-bootable aiueos whose node could DECIDE its murakumo.cloud participation and could not ACT on it, and named the reason as gap 1: aiueos had no network of any kind. PCI enumerated virtio RNG, BLK, INPUT and GPU; there was no NIC driver, no frame path, no stack. src/aiueos/net.cljc is 41 lines of URL allowlist for HOST adapters and had never touched a wire.

Reaching murakumo.cloud is a stack: link -> ARP/IPv4 -> TCP -> TLS -> HTTPS. Only the bottom of it is decided here. ADR-2608052000 closed gap 2 (the compiler native slice) in the same session; this closes the bottom layer of gap 1.")

  (:adr/decision
  "Implement the link layer, and prove it against a REAL PEER rather than a loopback.

- virtio-net driver in os/aiueos/kernel/pci.c, following the virtio-blk/rng pattern already there: modern PCI transport, VERSION_1 negotiation, two virtqueues (0 receive, 1 transmit). Every device before this one used exactly one queue, so `prepare_queue` selected queue 0 by construction; it gains an index parameter and the old single-queue signature delegates.
- The receive buffer is posted BEFORE DRIVER_OK, so a reply cannot arrive with no buffer to land in.
- Frame admission is a Kotoba object, not C: kotoba/net-arp-reply-valid.kotoba compiles to x86_64-aiueos-kernel-v1, exports kotoba_aiueos_net_arp_reply_valid through kotoba-native's reviewed allow-list, and passes the same fail-closed verifier as every other object (imports=0 relocations=1). C performs the bounded DMA and decides nothing -- the mechanism/decision split ADR-2607241100 defines.
- The exchange is ARP on purpose: the smallest thing that proves a peer answered -- 14 bytes of Ethernet, 28 of ARP, no IP stack anywhere. Under QEMU SLIRP the gateway 10.0.2.2 answers, with no host network access involved.
- Admission checks EVERY field of the reply, not just the opcode. A device model echoing our own broadcast back would pass an opcode-only check; admission requires opcode 2 (a reply, not the 1 we sent) AND sender IPv4 10.0.2.2.")

  (:adr/consequences
  "AIUEOS_VIRTIO_NET_OK modern-pci rx/tx arp-reply kotoba-admitted, alongside AIUEOS_UEFI_SMOKE_OK. aiueos transmits and receives real frames on bare metal, and the received one is admitted by compiler-emitted Kotoba.

A NIC is optional and stays optional. It is attached only under AIUEOS_TEST_NET=1, so every existing gate boots the machine it booted before, and the kernel reports AIUEOS_VIRTIO_NET_ABSENT no-nic-attached rather than failing when none is present. Measured both ways. An attached-but-broken device cannot pass as 'no network': presence is what is reported, and when present the exchange must have completed and been admitted.

THIS IS THE LINK LAYER AND NOTHING ABOVE IT. A node still cannot reach murakumo.cloud.

The driver SPINS and specifically must not `hlt`. It claims no MSI-X vector -- unlike rng and blk, which install one before going live -- so nothing would wake a sleeper, and `sti` unmasks a device interrupt nothing routes. Measured by building it: a variant copying the rng driver's `sti; hlt; cli` wedges the boot immediately after AIUEOS_APIC_TIMER_OK.

Worth recording how that was found. The host was at load average ~250 during testing, where a TCG boot takes many minutes and a wedge is indistinguishable from contention at a glance. The first diagnosis WAS 'contention', and it was wrong; reverting to the spin and re-running is what separated them. The gate's own 600s timeout can also fire spuriously under that load and is retried as the known flake (kotoba-lang/aiueos#108). A slow host does not just cost time here -- it actively produces wrong diagnoses.")

  (:adr/gaps
  "[{:gap :ip-and-above :state :partial :note \"IPv4 LANDED (aiueos ADR-0021) and TCP LANDED (aiueos ADR-0022), both 2026-08-05. IPv4: ARP cache + ICMP echo round trip, both checksums admitted by Kotoba. TCP: one connection completing handshake, payload, echo and close against a REAL peer -- QEMU guestfwd piping the stream through cat, so sequence numbers, ACKs and both checksums all have to be right or nothing comes back. AIUEOS_IPV4_OK + AIUEOS_TCP_OK alongside AIUEOS_UEFI_SMOKE_OK, exit 0. WHAT THESE ARE NOT: IPv4 has no fragmentation, routing table, ARP expiry or address configuration (SLIRP topology hardcoded). TCP is a PROBE, not a stack -- no retransmit timers, no congestion control, no window management beyond a fixed window, no reassembly, no multiple connections, no options; the rx ring holds one buffer so back-to-back segments drop the second; the echoed bytes are never compared with what was sent. STILL MISSING: TLS 1.3, then HTTPS. Until those exist a node cannot reach murakumo.cloud.\"}
    {:gap :net-interrupts :state :open :note \"The driver polls because it claims no MSI-X vector. Claiming one, and then being able to sleep instead of spin, is follow-on work -- rng and blk already show the shape.\"}
    {:gap :net-identity :state :open :note \"Fixed locally-administered MAC; VIRTIO_NET_F_MAC is not negotiated and nothing is read from device config space. A node needing a stable identity on a real network must negotiate it.\"}
    {:gap :real-hardware :state :unverified :note \"Proved under QEMU SLIRP only, like USB boot before it. No physical NIC has been driven.\"}
    {:gap :remaining-murakumo-cores :state :partial :note \"19 of 33 still do not compile natively (ADR-2608052000).\"}]")

  (:adr/evidence
  "aiueos 72f5204c3a97ce62d2793384a656b03454560596, kotoba-native d84b947b1af5b4edf0c05a241416723cb6eb9950, both on main and verified fast-forward before the west pins advanced (root 3015215f, 16a1866d).
NIC attached: AIUEOS_VIRTIO_NET_OK modern-pci rx/tx arp-reply kotoba-admitted; AIUEOS_UEFI_SMOKE_OK; smoke exit status 0.
No NIC: AIUEOS_VIRTIO_NET_ABSENT no-nic-attached; AIUEOS_UEFI_SMOKE_OK; smoke exit status 0.
Object admission at link time: AIUEOS_KOTOBA_OBJECT_OK target=x86_64-aiueos-kernel-v1 export=kotoba_aiueos_net_arp_reply_valid imports=0 relocations=1.
Digest pinned in build-uefi.sh: 63d9862b6a1b23561dd07bd79e76b65285c974b4d39a4cc56c676791a6dc1dfb.")

  (:adr/authoritative-for (vector "aiueos-link-layer" "aiueos-net-frame-admission"))
